# Arrays

`Array a` is a fixed-length sequence of values of type `a`, indexed from 0 ([D91](../DECISIONS.md)).
It is an immutable value like a list: `Array.set` returns a new array and leaves the old one as it
was. What makes it an array rather than a list is the cost: every operation is O(1) when the program
does not use an old version again, which is how array code is usually written.

This document is the spec. The API is below, then its laws as theorems (proved by `deus test`),
then the cost model and how each runtime meets it. `deus add docs/spec/ARRAY.mdl` adds the theorems
to a codebase; `deus mdl docs/spec/ARRAY.mdl` checks the requirements.

## API

The four primitives are declared in [`deus/builtins.deus`](../../deus/builtins.deus):

```text
Array.make   : Int -> a -> Array a            -- n copies of a value; n < 0 is a defect
Array.length : Array a -> Int
Array.get    : Array a -> Int -> a            -- an index outside 0 .. length is a defect
Array.set    : Array a -> Int -> a -> Array a -- a new array; an index outside 0 .. length is a defect
```

Arrays compare structurally (`==` and `compare` look at the items, in order) and print as `#[1, 2]`.
A value has no identity, so nothing can tell two equal arrays apart.

## Laws

Every law is a theorem over any array, index and value. The bounds are hypotheses because an index
outside the array is a defect, not a value. The prover's model of the `Array` primitives is these
same equations (its trusted base, [`deus/prove.deus`](../../deus/prove.deus)), so the first seven
are proved by evaluation; that checks each statement against the model, and lets later proofs about
array code use them. The kernels are checked against the laws by the tests at the end, and by the
tested theorems, which need a property of a whole array and run on random arrays.

```deus
namespace ArrayLaws

/-- `make` builds an array of the length asked for. -/
theorem lengthMake (n : Int) (x : Int) : 0 <= n -> Array.length (Array.make n x) = n := by
  simp

/-- Every item of a made array is the value it was made with. -/
theorem getMake (n : Int) (x : Int) (i : Int) : 0 <= i -> i < n -> Array.get (Array.make n x) i = x := by
  simp

/-- `set` keeps the length. -/
theorem lengthSet (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (v : Int) : 0 <= i -> i < Array.length a -> Array.length (Array.set a i v) = Array.length a := by
  simp

/-- Reading the index just written gives the value written. -/
theorem getSetSame (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (v : Int) : 0 <= i -> i < Array.length a -> Array.get (Array.set a i v) i = v := by
  simp

/-- Reading any other index gives what the old array had there. -/
theorem getSetOther (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (j : Int) (v : Int) : 0 <= i -> i < Array.length a -> i != j -> Array.get (Array.set a i v) j = Array.get a j := by
  auto

/-- A second write to the same index replaces the first. -/
theorem setSetSame (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (v : Int) (w : Int) : Array.set (Array.set a i v) i w = Array.set a i w := by
  simp

/-- Writing back what is already there gives an equal array. -/
theorem setGetSame (a : Array Int) (i : Int) : 0 <= i -> i < Array.length a -> Array.set a i (Array.get a i) = a := by
  simp

/-- The items of `a` from `i` down to 0, onto `acc`. -/
def itemsGo (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (acc : List Int) : List Int :=
  if i < 0 then acc else itemsGo a (i - 1) (Array.get a i :: acc)

def items (a : Array Int) : List Int := itemsGo a (Array.length a - 1) []

/-- Two arrays are equal exactly when their items are (extensionality). -/
theorem equalItems (a : Array Int) (b : Array Int) : (a == b) = (items a == items b) := by test 200

/-- `i` folded into `0 .. n` (for `n > 0`), so a random index lands inside a random array. -/
def wrap (i : Int) (n : Int) : Int := (i % n + n) % n

/-- Writes to two different indices give the same array in either order. -/
def commutes (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (j : Int) (v : Int) (w : Int) : Bool :=
  let n := Array.length a
  if n == 0 then true
  else
    let x := wrap i n
    let y := wrap j n
    x == y || Array.set (Array.set a x v) y w == Array.set (Array.set a y w) x v

/-- Writes to different indices commute. -/
theorem setsCommute (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (j : Int) (v : Int) (w : Int) : commutes a i j v w = true := by test 200

/-- A write leaves the old array as it was (persistence): what the kernels' versions must keep true. -/
def keepsOld (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (v : Int) : Bool :=
  let n := Array.length a
  if n == 0 then true
  else
    let x := wrap i n
    let before := items a
    let b := Array.set a x v
    let c := Array.set b x (v + 1)
    items a == before && Array.get b x == v && Array.get c x == v + 1 && items b != items c && items a == before

theorem oldUnchanged (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (v : Int) : keepsOld a i v = true := by test 200

/-- `n` writes in a row, each to the newest array: the use the cost model makes O(1) per write. -/
def fillGo (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (n : Int) : Array Int :=
  if i >= n then a else fillGo (Array.set a i (n - i)) (i + 1) n

def fill (n : Int) : Array Int := fillGo (Array.make n 0) 0 n

/-- The proved laws, at every pair of indices of one array: the kernels agree with the prover's model. -/
def lawsAt (a : Array Int) (i : Int) (j : Int) (v : Int) : Bool :=
  let b := Array.set a i v
  Array.length b == Array.length a && Array.get b i == v && (i == j || Array.get b j == Array.get a j) && Array.set b i (v + 1) == Array.set a i (v + 1) && Array.set a i (Array.get a i) == a

test lawsHoldOnKernels :=
  let indices := List.range 0 5
  Array.length (Array.make 3 7) == 3 && List.all (fun i => Array.get (Array.make 3 7) i == 7) (List.range 0 3) && List.all (fun i => List.all (fun j => lawsAt (fill 5) i j 42) indices) indices

/-- Old versions stay readable while new ones are made from them. -/
test versionsIndependent :=
  let a := fill 4
  let b := Array.set a 0 9
  let c := Array.set a 0 7
  let d := Array.set b 3 1
  items a == [4, 3, 2, 1] && items b == [9, 3, 2, 1] && items c == [7, 3, 2, 1] && items d == [9, 3, 2, 1] && items (Array.set d 3 5) == [9, 3, 2, 5] && items a == [4, 3, 2, 1]

end ArrayLaws
```

## Cost model

| Operation              | Cost                                                                                 |
| ---------------------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------ |
| `make n x`             | O(n)                                                                                 |
| `length`, `get`, `set` | O(1) on the newest array, the one no `set` has been applied to yet                   |
| `get`, `set` on an older array | `get` O(1) compiled, `set` O(n) (it copies); O(k) on the kernels, for an array k writes old |

A program that only ever writes to the newest array, as every in-place algorithm does, pays O(1)
for each operation on every runtime. A program that keeps old versions still gets correct answers
and pays for what it keeps.

Each runtime meets this in its own way:

- **The kernels** (TypeScript and Rust) keep versions, Baker's trick with Conchon and Filliâtre's
  rerooting. The newest version owns the items and each older version is a diff to the next one.
  Reading an older version moves the items back to it first, so a program that alternates between
  two versions pays for the distance each time.
- **Compiled wasm** (`--wasm`) has one block of memory per array and never frees. A static pass,
  [`deus/unique.deus`](../../deus/unique.deus), finds every `set` whose array nothing will read
  again and makes it write in place. Every other `set` copies the array first. The pass is
  described in [WASM.md](WASM.md#unique-arrays).
- **C** (`--c`, and wasm built through it) counts references (Perceus). A `set` on an array the
  code holds the only reference to writes in place; on a shared one it copies, and on a borrowed
  one (a local still used later) it copies too.

## Extensions

| Term   | URI                      |
| ------ | ------------------------ |
| `perf` | `org.dxos.deus.perf@1.0` |

## Requirements

Writing every item once, each time to the newest array, is linear on the kernels:

```mdl
perf ARRAY-1: a run of writes to the newest array is linear
  run: fun n => ArrayLaws.fill n
  sizes: 100 | 200 | 400
  order: n
```
